I guess that the root cause is about oomkiller.

Based on the log from [containerd log](https://storage.googleapis.com/kubernetes-jenkins/pr-logs/pull/91651/pull-kubernetes-e2e-kind/1267615032988930051/artifacts/logs/kind-worker2/containerd.log)

When that `/bin/false` pod start, but it has been killed.

```
02 00:55:36 kind-worker2 containerd[128]: time="2020-06-02T00:55:36.149486402Z" level=error msg="add cg to OOM monitor" error="open /sys/fs/cgroup/memory/kubepods/burstable/pod553b0f48-8dbd-4ff6-8e4c-289a0a9b7f5c/583c17764ed521bc6e30482e10faff6f0cda6d5351cfdb9b942b905df0eec592/memory.oom_control: no such file or directory"

Jun 02 00:55:36 kind-worker2 containerd[128]: time="2020-06-02T00:55:36.157731626Z" level=info msg="StartContainer for \"583c17764ed521bc6e30482e10faff6f0cda6d5351cfdb9b942b905df0eec592\" returns successfully"

Jun 02 00:55:36 kind-worker2 containerd[128]: time="2020-06-02T00:55:36.158443064Z" level=info msg="TaskExit event &TaskExit{ContainerID:583c17764ed521bc6e30482e10faff6f0cda6d5351cfdb9b942b905df0eec592,ID:583c17764ed521bc6e30482e10faff6f0cda6d5351cfdb9b942b905df0eec592,Pid:195311,ExitStatus:137,ExitedAt:2020-06-02 00:55:36.131145685 +0000 UTC,XXX_unrecognized:[],}"
```

The log is interesting because that `StartContainer` does successfully but we received the exit event with exit status 137. If I understand correctly, `/bin/false` return exit status 1 and 137 is like oom killer.

And the tricky part  is first log line above. `/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/kubepods/burstable/pod553b0f48-8dbd-4ff6-8e4c-289a0a9b7f5c/583c17764ed521bc6e30482e10faff6f0cda6d5351cfdb9b942b905df0eec592/memory.oom_control` is not found.

The containerd `v1.4.0-beta-6312b52d` is using `1.0.0~rc10` runC. runC might destroy the cgroup path during initialization at two places
* https://github.com/opencontainers/runc/blob/v1.0.0-rc10/libcontainer/process_linux.go#L304-L310
* https://github.com/opencontainers/runc/blob/v1.0.0-rc10/libcontainer/process_linux.go#L353-L358

When cgroup path has been deleted, the `runc kill --all` will buggy us because it doesn't check the status.

```go
// https://github.com/opencontainers/runc/blob/v1.0.0-rc10/libcontainer//container_linux.go#L381

func (c *linuxContainer) Signal(s os.Signal, all bool) error {
        if all {
                return signalAllProcesses(c.cgroupManager, s). <----- no check status 
        }
        status, err := c.currentStatus()
        if err != nil {
                return err
        }
        // to avoid a PID reuse attack
        if status == Running || status == Created || status == Paused {
                if err := c.initProcess.signal(s); err != nil {
                        return newSystemErrorWithCause(err, "signaling init process")
                }
                return nil
        }
        return newGenericError(fmt.Errorf("container not running"), ContainerNotRunning)
}

// https://github.com/opencontainers/runc/blob/v1.0.0-rc10/libcontainer/init_linux.go#L470

// signalAllProcesses freezes then iterates over all the processes inside the
// manager's cgroups sending the signal s to them.
// If s is SIGKILL then it will wait for each process to exit.
// For all other signals it will check if the process is ready to report its
// exit status and only if it is will a wait be performed.
func signalAllProcesses(m cgroups.Manager, s os.Signal) error {
        var procs []*os.Process
        if err := m.Freeze(configs.Frozen); err != nil {
                logrus.Warn(err).  <------------ just warning
        }
        pids, err := m.GetAllPids(). <----- check devices path and oops
        if err != nil {
                m.Freeze(configs.Thawed)
                return err
        }
        for _, pid := range pids {
                p, err := os.FindProcess(pid)
                if err != nil {
                        logrus.Warn(err)
                        continue
                }
                procs = append(procs, p)
                if err := p.Signal(s); err != nil {
                        logrus.Warn(err)
                }
        }
```

I didn't know why runC destroys the cgroupPath. No clue yet.
But I think runC should cover the cgrouppath-404 case.
Hope it can help

